Head Lice (Pediculosis Capitis)
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Intense scalp pruritus, Nits firmly attached to hair shafts, Live lice visualized

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-021
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe scalp excoriation with secondary bleeding and uncontrollable psychosocial distress resulting in complete insomnia.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Mother
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a pediatric patient with intense, relentless scalp pruritus causing violent scratching, leading to active scalp bleeding and secondary bacterial infection (crusting). The severe irritability and complete sleep disturbance of both patient and caregiver mandated urgent out-of-hours assessment to achieve symptom control, rule out severe secondary impetigo, and initiate immediate pediculocidal therapy, preventing further tissue damage and profound distress prior to morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "Her head is covered in bugs and she has scratched herself until she's bleeding. None of us have slept a wink for two nights."
History of Present Illness: The patient began complaining of a sensation of something moving in the hair 5 days ago, accompanied by mild itching. Over the past 48 hours, this escalated into intense, relentless scalp pruritus. The mother reports visualizing live crawling lice on the scalp this evening. The severe itching has provoked violent, continuous scratching, resulting in multiple painful excoriations on the scalp and posterior neck. The patient exhibits extreme irritability and complete sleep disturbance. No history of topical pediculicide use in the current outbreak.
Functional Status: Independent, currently agitated and sleep-deprived.
Social/Safety Concerns: High communicability risk within the household; severe caregiver anxiety and fatigue.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 | HR: 95 | Temp: 37.1 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 28 Kg
General: Agitated, tearful, continuously digging fingers into the scalp.
Physical Exam: Scalp examination reveals multiple firmly attached oval white nits on the proximal hair shafts, localized predominantly behind the ears and at the nape of the neck. Direct clinical visualization confirms multiple live, motile lice near the scalp surface. The posterior neck and occipital scalp display extensive excoriations with focal serosanguinous crusting indicative of early secondary bacterial colonization. Tender posterior cervical lymphadenopathy is palpable bilaterally. No fluctuant abscesses or extensive purulent discharge.
Environmental Observations: Overcrowded sleeping arrangements noted, facilitating transmission.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Pediculosis Capitis with secondary excoriation - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Seborrheic Dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of live crawling insects and nits firmly cemented to the hair shaft; seborrheic scale is easily detached and lacks the intense, acute mechanical pruritus.

Tinea Capitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of localized areas of alopecia, broken hairs, or classic fungal scaling; visualization of live lice confirms parasitic etiology.

Allergic Contact Dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Direct evidence of lice/nits, and lack of an external chemical trigger or generalized weeping erythema.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents in acute distress with severe pediculosis capitis complicated by significant excoriation and early secondary bacterial infection. Triage accurately identified the active bleeding, uncontrollable scratching, and severe caregiver panic as an acute crisis requiring immediate intervention. Delaying assessment until daytime would have resulted in continued self-harm to the scalp, worsening secondary infection, and prolonged extreme psychological distress for the family. Immediate out-of-hours evaluation allowed for definitive diagnosis, immediate physical soothing of the excoriations, and the commencement of targeted topical treatment and oral antihistamines to rapidly break the itch cycle and permit sleep.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed topical Permethrin 1% lotion to be applied strictly as directed. Prescribed oral Cetirizine 5mg stat and daily for pruritus control. Advised a short course of topical Mupirocin 2% ointment to the heavily excoriated and crusted areas twice daily.
Positive Behavioral Change: By applying the medicated lotion exactly to the scalp and leaving it for the recommended 10 minutes, you will rapidly paralyze and kill the adult lice, immediately stopping the crawling sensation. By taking the anti-itch medicine tonight and keeping your fingernails cut extremely short, you will stop tearing your skin, allowing the bleeding scratches to heal painlessly over the next few days.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Fine-toothed metal nit comb recommended.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days to ensure successful eradication and resolution of the secondary crusting.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the scratched areas on the scalp become deeply red, hot, swollen, or begin draining thick yellow pus, or if the patient develops a fever, present to the nearest Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Eradication requires strict adherence to the treatment protocol, including environmental decontamination.
Clinical Course: The intense itching should begin to subside within 24 hours of the first treatment and antihistamine use. A second application in 7-10 days is mandatory to kill newly hatched nymphs.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Monitor the crusted scratches closely. Seek medical care if redness spreads down the neck or a fever develops, indicating a bacterial infection.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The bugs are biting the scalp to feed, which causes a severe allergic reaction and intense itching. The lotion kills the live bugs, the comb removes the eggs, and the ointment prevents the open scratches from becoming infected.
Actionable Steps: Wash all bedding and recently worn clothing in hot water. Treat all affected household members simultaneously to prevent immediate reinfection.

